  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "V1g2IbP/YlwZoScF7JBgzcKYJRw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:36:07.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The host applications \u2014 Connect, Switchboard, Fusion, Renown \u2014 are infrastructure. On top of them Powerhouse launched two named platforms whose stated purpose is to demonstrate what the tech stack can build:\n\n- **Vetra Builder Platform** (vetra.io) \u2014 \"sovereign infrastructure for scalable network organizations \u2014 local first, built to scale.\" This is the builder-facing platform: where document models, editors, processors, and packages are specified, generated, and published.\n- **Achra Decentralized Operations Platform** (achra.com) \u2014 \"where open organizations can procure and purchase services.\" This is the operations-facing platform: the surface on which organizations scope work, choose providers, and transact.\n\n## Why the pairing matters\n\nThe two platforms are the concrete instantiation of a distinction that runs through Powerhouse's strategy documents: a builder platform that governs *creation* of reusable modules, and an operations platform that runs *daily coordination*. The abstract pair is discussed frequently; the names attached to them are easy to miss. Vetra is the builder platform. Achra is the decentralized operations platform. If you have read about \"the Builder Platform\" and \"the Decentralized Operations Platform\" as roles, these are the products filling them.\n\n## How to read \"demonstration\"\n\nNeither is positioned as the product being sold. They exist to show that the stack \u2014 Reactor, Connect, Switchboard, Fusion, Renown \u2014 composes into real platforms, and to serve as the reference implementation others fork or extend for their own network organizations. Vetra in particular doubles as the builder toolchain Powerhouse itself develops with, which is why its name also attaches to concrete developer surfaces (Vetra Studio, the Vetra registry, Vetra Cloud) rather than only to a platform brand.\n\nBoth sit on the same substrate: Vetra builds and publishes the modules; Achra is one kind of organization-facing platform assembled from them.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/00-Architecture/00-PowerhouseArchitecture.md \u2014 unchanged since the v6.0.0-dev.254 baseline, so this is older material that remains valid rather than freshly re-verified v6.2.2 content._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:36:07.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:36:07.000Z"
    }
  },
